 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed independent of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookup resolves against a local embedded SQLite index that each kiosk builds from the active snapshot during import. The index is private to this node; no certificate behavior or interface depends on it.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the SQLite index for the new snapshot in a staging location, then activate it only after the signature check, schema check, uniqueness validation, and index build have all succeeded. Until activation, in-progress records are never exposed to lookup. An import failure — including a failure to build the index — leaves the previous snapshot and its index active and reports a useful error.
+
+Activation advances a single active-index pointer to the staged index; the previous index is retained until the pointer advances, so a lookup always resolves against one complete index.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Apply the same normalization at index-build time and at query time: strip surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters, leaving serial-number punctuation significant. A lookup is then a single indexed match of the normalized serial against the active index. Return the matching record with the active snapshot date, or `NotFound` with the active snapshot date when no row matches.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, built on each kiosk from the active snapshot | On-site HTTPS directory service; managed search service | The no-runtime-network tenet and the no-cloud-services non-goal eliminate both networked options at lookup time, since each resolves a lookup over the lab network or external connectivity. A file-local SQLite index keeps lookups on-kiosk and dependency-free; the accepted cost is that every kiosk validates the snapshot and rebuilds its own index on import. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. **Uniqueness scope under normalization.** REG-003 rejects duplicate serial numbers, but records whose serials differ only in surrounding whitespace or ASCII letter case collapse to one normalized key. Whether uniqueness is enforced on raw serials (two such records import, but both compete for one normalized key at lookup) or on normalized serials (the collision is rejected at import) is unresolved and changes both import validation and lookup determinism.
+2. **Activation crash recovery.** The exact mechanism that guarantees a crash during pointer advance leaves exactly one complete active index (e.g., pointer file plus atomic rename) is unspecified.
+3. **Disk headroom and cleanup.** A kiosk transiently holds the active index plus the staging index during import. Required free space and the point at which a superseded index is removed after activation are unspecified.
+4. **Build memory bound.** Index build must stream records to stay within 4 GB RAM for snapshots up to 150 MB rather than loading all records at once.
